{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_001 argues a low-variance verification process replaces high-variance expert intuition, but its own counterconditions expose the missing causal link: if non-experts lack the domain knowledge to interpret the checklist, the 'low-variance' process yields uniformly wrong answers rather than reliability. The target never supplies a causal mechanism for how a non-expert panel transitions from a checklist's text to an objectively correct outcome; it merely assumes that following a checklist mechanically raises a 'reliability floor,' which is precisely the compliance behavior the problem asks to distinguish from genuine reliability. The surgical-checklist analogy is even self-defeating on this point (checklists used by experts, not non-experts), leaving no causal account tying non-expert verification to correctness.","target":"192a4b5729fb0ae61b2033f4f890cbd35710a203c44b52b39b65487a58d32d38"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_002's 'Plausible Decoy' mechanism lacks a causal link to reliability: a decoy that is 'logically consistent with the process but irrelevant to the correct outcome' tests whether a panel will reject an instruction, but rejecting instructions is neither necessary nor sufficient for objectively verifiable reliability. A panel that blindly trusts expert-built checklists can be highly reliable yet 'fail' the decoy, while a non-expert panel that rejects the decoy for the wrong idiosyncratic reason can score high on the probe while failing real items. SRC_002 asserts that rejecting a plausible decoy is 'the hallmark of reliability over obedience,' but it provides no causal mechanism mapping decoy rejection to lower error rate on the target task. Its own counterconditions concede both experts being fooled and instructions forbidding questioning, undermining any task-reliability inference.","counterexample":[{"decoy_rejection":"true","outcome":"objective","panel_compliant":"accepts decoy but correctly follows all task-valid checklist items","panel_non_expert":"rejects decoy (epistemic agency) but fails verifiable items due to lack of domain knowledge","reliability":"low","reliability_alt":"high"}],"target":"71f4c6f0a5e02c229f15ff6892f5fe6df5db12ce0b18a7bc16a427ffdae8aa3c"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_003's 'decoy/canary' analogy does not supply a causal mechanism: in software a canary validates that a process is executing (formal validity), but the problem asks for reliability on tasks with objectively verifiable outcomes (substantive correctness). A panel correctly rejecting a decoy item shows it can catch an injected anomaly, but SRC_003 never establishes that the same disposition causes fewer errors on the genuine items—'understanding' the decoy need not cause 'understanding' the task. The target even admits the disanalogy (humans rationalize decoys as special cases), so the transferred mechanism can misfire without the panel's reliability changing at all. Its own overturn condition (panel trained to spot decoys) concedes the probe can measure training, not reliability, breaking the inferential link the problem demands.","counterexample":[{"causal_link":"none","detection_method":"decoy rejection","measured_signal":"decoy rejection = reliability","outcome":"objective_correctness","panel_non_expert":"detects injected decoy (trained pattern) but still errs on verifiable items due to domain-knowledge gap","underlying_reliability":"low","verdict":"reliable"}],"target":"8de11001d1171cb645660efb90bc6d6daac8d4638546686f3236ec7526aaabbb"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_004's 'Justification Delta' mechanism conflates richer justification with causal reliability: requiring grounded traces ('Item 4 is present because [Observation X]') is satisfied by any plausible-sounding explanation, so the mechanism cannot distinguish genuine reliability from templated rationalization. The target concedes this in a countercondition (panel trained to fake justifications using a template), but does not supply any causal process tying longer/more-specific justifications to higher objective accuracy. For binary/obvious items, which the problem does not exclude, the justification delta is degenerate; and for subjective-appearing verifiable tasks, verbose traces can correlate negatively with reliability when non-experts confabulate. Thus the proposed evidence does not causally discriminate reliability from compliance.","counterexample":[{"justification_delta":"large","justification_delta_alt":"small","outcome":"objective","panel_compliant":"produces grounded traces from template: 'Item 4 is present because [Observation X]'","panel_reliable":"answers correctly with minimal binary signal","reliability":"compliant but wrong on verifiable outcome","reliability_alt":"high"}],"target":"e6a5c406b30ff39e0a77d2ccf5d8e669d6c770bb5704aba88e88b24b3630f63a"}]}